text:
This document walks through a complete Bayesian workflow, from the first question to a result we can defend.
Imagine you’re in charge of admissions at a large university. You want to know if your admissions process is fair. You’ve got data from thousands of individual applicants. What now?
The workflow has eight steps:

Define the estimand. State exactly what you want to know before you touch the data.
Draw the causal model (DAG). Write down what you assume causes what, based on what you know about the domain.
Simulate the population. Write a simulation that could produce data like yours.
Write the formal model. Derive the statistical model from the DAG, in math notation.
Write the Stan code. Translate the formal model into a program.
Test on simulated data. Check that the priors imply plausible data and that the model recovers parameters we already know.
Fit and validate the model. Run the sampler, check its diagnostics, and run posterior predictive checks. Can we trust the model?
Compute the estimand and test its robustness. What is the result, and how much does it depend on our assumptions?

The steps are numbered, but the workflow is a loop. If the model can’t recover known parameters in step 6, or can’t reproduce the data in step 7, go back and fix the model, the simulation, or the DAG. Each of those failures teaches us something.


Show code
# libraries
library(tidyverse)
library(cmdstanr)
library(tidybayes)
library(posterior)
library(bayesplot)

library(rlang)

replace_indexes &lt;- function(tbl, data, ...) {
  specs &lt;- rlang::enquos(...)
  factor_levels &lt;- lapply(data, levels)

  parse_spec &lt;- function(q) {
    expr &lt;- rlang::get_expr(q)

    if (rlang::is_symbol(expr)) {
      return(list(var = rlang::as_string(expr), dims = NULL))
    }

    if (rlang::is_call(expr, "[")) {
      var &lt;- rlang::as_string(expr[[2]])
      dims &lt;- vapply(as.list(expr)[-c(1, 2)], rlang::as_string, character(1))
      return(list(var = var, dims = dims))
    }

    stop("Invalid specification")
  }

  parsed &lt;- lapply(specs, parse_spec)
  out &lt;- tbl

  for (p in parsed) {
    var &lt;- p$var
    dims &lt;- p$dims
    if (is.null(dims)) {
      next
    }

    idx_pattern &lt;- paste(rep("(\\d+)", length(dims)), collapse = ",")
    full_pattern &lt;- paste0("^", var, "\\[", idx_pattern, "\\]$")

    out$variable &lt;- vapply(
      out$variable,
      function(v) {
        if (!grepl(paste0("^", var, "\\["), v)) {
          return(v)
        }

        m &lt;- stringr::str_match(v, full_pattern)
        if (all(is.na(m))) {
          return(v)
        }

        idx &lt;- as.integer(m[1, -1])

        labels &lt;- Map(
          function(dim, i) {
            lvls &lt;- factor_levels[[dim]]
            if (is.null(lvls) || i &gt; length(lvls)) {
              return(NA_character_)
            }
            lvls[i]
          },
          dims,
          idx
        )

        paste0(var, "[", paste(unlist(labels), collapse = ","), "]")
      },
      character(1)
    )
  }

  out
}

# Draw a DAG as a ggplot: a circle per variable, an arrow per direct cause.
#   edges:      c("S -&gt; H", "H -&gt; W")
#   pos:        list(S = c(0, 0), H = c(1, 1), W = c(2, 0))
#   labels:     full names drawn beside each node, e.g. c(S = "Sex")
#   above:      nodes whose label goes above the circle instead of below
#   highlight:  edges to draw in red (e.g. one path); the rest fade to gray
#   exposure, outcome, unobserved: node names to style
dag_plot &lt;- function(
  edges,
  pos,
  labels = NULL,
  above = NULL,
  highlight = NULL,
  exposure = NULL,
  outcome = NULL,
  unobserved = NULL,
  r = 0.3,
  text_size = 9
) {
  ink &lt;- "#16314d"
  blue &lt;- "#1b91ff"
  red &lt;- "#ff2c2d"
  gray &lt;- "#999999"

  nodes &lt;- tibble::tibble(
    name = names(pos),
    x = vapply(pos, `[`, numeric(1), 1),
    y = vapply(pos, `[`, numeric(1), 2)
  )
  nodes$fill &lt;- ifelse(
    nodes$name %in% exposure,
    blue,
    ifelse(nodes$name %in% outcome, ink, "white")
  )
  nodes$text &lt;- ifelse(nodes$fill == "white", ink, "white")
  nodes$linetype &lt;- ifelse(nodes$name %in% unobserved, "dashed", "solid")
  nodes$label &lt;- if (is.null(labels)) "" else unname(labels[nodes$name])
  nodes$label[is.na(nodes$label)] &lt;- ""
  nodes$label_y &lt;- ifelse(nodes$name %in% above, nodes$y + r + 0.12, nodes$y - r - 0.12)
  nodes$vjust &lt;- ifelse(nodes$name %in% above, 0, 1)

  circles &lt;- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(nrow(nodes)), function(i) {
    t &lt;- seq(0, 2 * pi, length.out = 61)
    data.frame(
      name = nodes$name[i],
      x = nodes$x[i] + r * cos(t),
      y = nodes$y[i] + r * sin(t),
      fill = nodes$fill[i],
      linetype = nodes$linetype[i]
    )
  }))

  # Arrows run between circle edges, not centers
  ends &lt;- strsplit(gsub("\\s", "", edges), "-&gt;")
  from &lt;- vapply(ends, `[`, character(1), 1)
  to &lt;- vapply(ends, `[`, character(1), 2)
  x0 &lt;- nodes$x[match(from, nodes$name)]
  y0 &lt;- nodes$y[match(from, nodes$name)]
  x1 &lt;- nodes$x[match(to, nodes$name)]
  y1 &lt;- nodes$y[match(to, nodes$name)]
  len &lt;- sqrt((x1 - x0)^2 + (y1 - y0)^2)
  gap &lt;- r + 0.04
  arrows &lt;- data.frame(
    x = x0 + (x1 - x0) * gap / len,
    y = y0 + (y1 - y0) * gap / len,
    xend = x1 - (x1 - x0) * gap / len,
    yend = y1 - (y1 - y0) * gap / len,
    color = if (is.null(highlight)) {
      ink
    } else {
      ifelse(paste0(from, "-&gt;", to) %in% gsub("\\s", "", highlight), red, gray)
    }
  )

  ggplot2::ggplot() +
    ggplot2::geom_segment(
      data = arrows,
      ggplot2::aes(x, y, xend = xend, yend = yend, color = I(color)),
      linewidth = 1.6,
      arrow = ggplot2::arrow(length = ggplot2::unit(0.18, "inches"), type = "closed")
    ) +
    ggplot2::geom_polygon(
      data = circles,
      ggplot2::aes(x, y, group = name, fill = I(fill), linetype = I(linetype)),
      color = ink,
      linewidth = 1.2
    ) +
    ggplot2::geom_text(
      data = nodes,
      ggplot2::aes(x, y, label = name, color = I(text)),
      size = text_size,
      fontface = "bold"
    ) +
    ggplot2::geom_text(
      data = nodes,
      ggplot2::aes(x, label_y, label = label, vjust = vjust),
      color = ink,
      size = text_size * 0.75
    ) +
    ggplot2::coord_equal(clip = "off") +
    ggplot2::scale_x_continuous(expand = ggplot2::expansion(add = 0.6)) +
    ggplot2::scale_y_continuous(expand = ggplot2::expansion(add = 0.6)) +
    ggplot2::theme_void()
}


# print formatting
options(scipen = 999)
set.seed(1)

# data import
df &lt;- read.csv("datasets/berkeley.csv") |&gt;
  mutate(
    sex = factor(sex, levels = c("male", "female")),
    dept = factor(dept)
  )



Step 1: Define the estimand
The estimand is the precise quantity we want to learn. “Is admissions fair?” is too vague to estimate. Before we write any code, we need to turn it into a number.
Suppose we find that women are admitted at a lower rate overall. That gap could come from two places:

A direct effect: departments treat women differently from men who apply to the same department.
An indirect effect: women apply to different departments, and those departments admit fewer people.

Both are worth knowing about, but they call for different fixes. As the admissions office, we control the decisions departments make, so we’ll focus on the direct effect:

Estimand: the average difference in admission probability between women and men who apply to the same department, averaged across departments in proportion to how many people apply to each.

We’ll measure it as a difference in admission rates. For example, a result of −0.05 would mean women are admitted 5 percentage points less often than men who apply to the same department. Settling this first tells us what the model has to include and what we’ll compute from it at the end.


Step 2: Draw the causal model (DAG)
A causal model contains no numbers. It’s a picture of the variables and of which ones cause which. You’ll hear it called a DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph), which is a complicated name for a simple idea.

dag { Admitted [outcome,pos="-0.619,0.077"] Department [exposure,pos="-0.858,0.192"] Sex [exposure,pos="-1.059,-0.272"] Department -&gt; Admitted Sex -&gt; Admitted Sex -&gt; Department }


How do you pick these variables? You use what you know about the domain. We include sex because sex-based discrimination is a major social issue. We include department because some departments are much harder to get into than others.
Each arrow is an assumption. Each missing arrow is an assumption too, and usually a stronger one. The arrow \(\text{Sex} \to \text{Admitted}\) is the direct effect. The path \(\text{Sex} \to \text{Department} \to \text{Admitted}\) is the indirect effect. The DAG tells us that to estimate the direct effect we need to condition on department, so department has to go into the model.
The DAG also leaves things out. Many other factors affect admission, like an applicant’s qualifications. Leaving them off the graph is fine as long as they aren’t related to sex. In other words, we’re assuming that the men and women who apply are equally qualified on average. That’s a strong assumption, and we’ll come back to it in Step 8.


Step 3: Simulate the population
Before writing a statistical model, we write a generative one: a simulation that follows the DAG and produces fake applicants. This makes us state precisely how we think the data come about. Later, it gives us data where we know the right answer.


Show code
depts &lt;- levels(df$dept)

# how selective each department is (baseline admission rates)
dept_rates &lt;- c(A = 0.65, B = 0.60, C = 0.35, D = 0.33, E = 0.25, F = 0.07)

# where men and women tend to apply (Sex -&gt; Department)
male_pref &lt;- c(0.30, 0.20, 0.12, 0.15, 0.07, 0.14)
female_pref &lt;- c(0.06, 0.02, 0.32, 0.20, 0.21, 0.19)

simulate_admissions &lt;- function(
  n = 4500,
  discrimination = 0, # direct effect of being female, in log-odds (Sex -&gt; Admitted)
  u_on_admit = 0, # how much qualifications matter for admission (not in the DAG)
  female_selection = 0 # how much more qualified female applicants are, in SDs (for Step 8)
) {
  tibble(
    sex = sample(c("male", "female"), n, replace = TRUE, prob = c(0.6, 0.4))
  ) |&gt;
    mutate(
      u = rnorm(n, mean = female_selection * (sex == "female")),
      dept = if_else(
        sex == "female",
        sample(depts, n, replace = TRUE, prob = female_pref),
        sample(depts, n, replace = TRUE, prob = male_pref)
      ),
      p = plogis(
        qlogis(dept_rates[dept]) +
          discrimination * (sex == "female") +
          u_on_admit * u
      ),
      admitted = rbinom(n, 1, p),
      sex = factor(sex, levels = c("male", "female")),
      dept = factor(dept, levels = depts)
    ) |&gt;
    select(dept, sex, u, admitted)
}

# the true value of the estimand in a simulated population: switch each applicant's sex,
# holding their department and qualifications fixed
true_direct_effect &lt;- function(sim, discrimination, u_on_admit = 0) {
  sim |&gt;
    mutate(
      base = qlogis(dept_rates[as.character(dept)]) + u_on_admit * u,
      diff = plogis(base + discrimination) - plogis(base)
    ) |&gt;
    group_by(dept) |&gt;
    summarise(n = n(), diff = mean(diff)) |&gt;
    summarise(sum(n / sum(n) * diff)) |&gt;
    pull()
}


Let’s simulate a world with no discrimination at all: within every department, men and women are admitted at the same rate. Women just tend to apply to more selective departments.


Show code
sim_fair &lt;- simulate_admissions(discrimination = 0)

# overall admission rates by sex
sim_fair |&gt;
  group_by(sex) |&gt;
  summarise(applicants = n(), admit_rate = mean(admitted))







Women are admitted much less often overall, even though the simulation contains no discrimination. The whole gap comes from the indirect path through department. This is why the estimand has to be defined within departments, and why department belongs in the model. If we compared overall rates, we’d find a gap that isn’t there.


Step 4: Write the formal model
Next, we turn the DAG into a statistical model:
\[
\begin{align}
\text{admitted}_i &\sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(p_i) \\
\mathrm{logit}(p_i)
&= \alpha
+ \beta_{\mathrm{sex}[i]}
+ \gamma_{\mathrm{dept}[i]} \\
\alpha &\sim \mathrm{Normal}(0, 1.5) \\
\beta_{\mathrm{sex}} &\sim \mathrm{Normal}(0, 1) \\
\gamma_{\mathrm{dept}} &\sim \mathrm{Normal}(0, 1) \\
0 &= \textstyle\sum_{\mathrm{sex}} \beta = \sum_{\mathrm{dept}} \gamma
\end{align}
\]
It looks complicated, but it has four parts:

A likelihood (line 1): how randomness enters. Each applicant is admitted or not.
A linear predictor (line 2): how the arrows in the DAG combine. There’s a baseline \(\alpha\), a sex effect \(\beta\), and a department effect \(\gamma\).
Priors (lines 3–5): what we believe before we see the data.
Constraints (line 6): each set of effects adds up to zero. Without this, the model couldn’t tell its parts apart. Adding 1 to \(\alpha\) and subtracting 1 from every \(\gamma\) would give exactly the same predictions. With the constraint, \(\alpha\) is the overall average and every other term is a deviation from it.

Each term in the model matches a piece of the DAG. Sex and department both point into admission, so both appear in the linear predictor. The estimand from Step 1 comes from \(\beta\), the term that lets women’s and men’s chances differ within a department.
The model also makes a simplifying assumption: the sex effect \(\beta\) is the same in every department. That keeps the model small. We’ll find out in Step 7 whether the data go along with it.


Step 5: Write the Stan code
Now we translate the formal model into a program the computer can run:


models/berkeley.stan

data {
  // dimensions
  int&lt;lower=1&gt; n;
  int&lt;lower=1&gt; n_sex;
  int&lt;lower=1&gt; n_dept;

  // observed outcomes
  array[n] int&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; admitted;

  // group indices
  array[n] int&lt;lower=1, upper=n_sex&gt; sex;
  array[n] int&lt;lower=1, upper=n_dept&gt; dept;

  // prior predictive toggle (skips likelihood calculations)
  int&lt;lower=0, upper=1&gt; prior_only;
}
parameters {
  real alpha;
  sum_to_zero_vector[n_sex] beta;
  sum_to_zero_vector[n_dept] gamma;
}
model {
  alpha ~ normal(0, 1.5);
  // the sum-to-zero constraints shrink each effect's spread; widening the scales
  // undoes that, so each effect's prior SD matches the formal model (1)
  beta ~ normal(0, 1 * inv_sqrt(1 - inv(n_sex)));
  gamma ~ normal(0, 1 * inv_sqrt(1 - inv(n_dept)));

  if (!prior_only) {
    vector[n] eta;
    for (i in 1 : n)
      eta[i] = alpha + beta[sex[i]] + gamma[dept[i]];
    admitted ~ bernoulli_logit(eta);
  }
}
generated quantities {
  // admission probability for every sex x department cell
  matrix[n_sex, n_dept] p;
  for (s in 1 : n_sex)
    for (d in 1 : n_dept)
      p[s, d] = inv_logit(alpha + beta[s] + gamma[d]);
}

A few details:

The priors are written directly in the model block, exactly as in the formal model.
Stan enforces the constraints with sum_to_zero_vector. Forcing effects to sum to zero shrinks their spread, so the code widens each prior’s scale to compensate. That way, each effect’s prior matches the formal model.
The prior_only switch skips the likelihood. With it on, the model samples from the priors alone, which is what we need for prior predictive checks.
generated quantities reports the admission probability for every sex × department cell. We’ll compute the estimand from these.

We compile the model and write a small helper so every fit in this document uses the same settings:


Show code
berkeley &lt;- cmdstan_model("models/berkeley.stan", dir = ".stancache")

fit_model &lt;- function(data, prior_only = 0, model = berkeley) {
  data |&gt;
    select(dept, sex, admitted) |&gt;
    compose_data() |&gt;
    c(list(prior_only = prior_only)) |&gt;
    model$sample(seed = 2026, refresh = 0, show_messages = FALSE)
}

# the estimand from Step 1, computed for every posterior draw
direct_effect &lt;- function(fit, data = df) {
  weights &lt;- data |&gt;
    count(dept) |&gt;
    mutate(w = n / sum(n)) |&gt;
    pull(w)

  fit |&gt;
    spread_draws(p[sex, dept]) |&gt;
    mutate(sex = levels(data$sex)[sex]) |&gt;
    pivot_wider(names_from = sex, values_from = p) |&gt;
    mutate(w = weights[dept]) |&gt;
    group_by(.draw) |&gt;
    summarise(direct_effect = sum(w * (female - male)))
}




Step 6: Test on simulated data
We test the model on data where we already know the answer before we trust it on data where we don’t. There are two checks.

Prior predictive check
First, do the priors imply plausible data? We run the model with prior_only = 1 and look at the admission rates it expects before seeing any data.


Show code
fit_prior &lt;- fit_model(df, prior_only = 1)




Show code
fit_prior |&gt;
  spread_draws(p[sex, dept]) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = p)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 40, fill = "#1b91ff", color = "white") +
  labs(
    x = "Admission probability for a sex × department cell",
    y = "Prior draws",
    title = "What the priors imply about admission rates"
  )









Show code
direct_effect(fit_prior) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = direct_effect)) +
  stat_halfeye(fill = "#1b91ff") +
  labs(
    x = "Direct effect (female − male)",
    y = NULL,
    title = "Prior on the estimand"
  )









The prior on the direct effect is centered on zero, so it doesn’t assume discrimination in either direction. It’s also wide: gaps of 50 percentage points or more either way are allowed.
The admission rates show a problem. The priors pile up near 0% and 100%. That happens because three Normal priors add up on the log-odds scale, and a log-odds of ±3 is already 5% or 95%. These priors are looser than our actual knowledge: we don’t believe departments admit nearly everyone or nearly no one. This is a judgment call. We could tighten \(\alpha\) and \(\gamma\) now. With thousands of applicants, we expect the data to outweigh the priors, so we’ll keep them and check that expectation directly in Step 8.


Parameter recovery
Second, can the model find an effect we put there ourselves? We simulate three populations: one with no discrimination, one where women are penalized by 0.5 on the log-odds scale, and one where they’re penalized by 1. We fit the model to each and check whether the posterior covers the true value of the estimand.


Show code
recovery &lt;- tibble(discrimination = c(0, -0.5, -1)) |&gt;
  mutate(
    sim = map(discrimination, \(b) simulate_admissions(discrimination = b)),
    truth = map2_dbl(sim, discrimination, true_direct_effect),
    estimate = map(sim, \(s) direct_effect(fit_model(s), data = s))
  )




Show code
recovery |&gt;
  select(discrimination, truth, estimate) |&gt;
  unnest(estimate) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = direct_effect, y = factor(discrimination))) +
  stat_halfeye(fill = "#1b91ff") +
  geom_point(
    data = distinct(recovery, discrimination, truth),
    aes(x = truth),
    color = "red",
    shape = 4,
    size = 4,
    stroke = 1.5
  ) +
  labs(
    x = "Direct effect (female − male)",
    y = "Discrimination built into the simulation (log-odds)",
    title = "Can the model recover a known effect?",
    subtitle = "Red ×: the true value of the estimand"
  )









In each case, the true value falls inside the posterior. The model and the estimand code work, at least in a world that matches our assumptions. If they didn’t, we’d have a bug or an identification problem, and we’d fix it here, where we know the right answer. Any single simulated dataset is noisy, so a more thorough version of this check repeats it many times with random true values. That’s called simulation-based calibration.



Step 7: Fit and validate the model
Now we fit the model to the real data.


Show code
fit &lt;- fit_model(df)



Sampler diagnostics
Before we look at any results, we check whether the sampler worked. Divergent transitions mean the sampler hit geometry it couldn’t explore. \(\hat{R}\) should be very close to 1, which means the chains agree with each other. Effective sample size (ESS) should be in the hundreds or more.


