Show code
mcmc_trace(fit$draws(c("alpha", "beta", "gamma")))









The trace plots should look like “fuzzy caterpillars”: chains that overlap and wander around the same region with no trends.


Posterior predictive check
Can the fitted model reproduce the data it was fit to? For each posterior draw, we simulate a new set of admissions for every sex × department cell and compare the result to what we actually observed.


Show code
cells &lt;- df |&gt;
  group_by(sex, dept) |&gt;
  summarise(applicants = n(), observed = mean(admitted), .groups = "drop")

fit |&gt;
  spread_draws(p[sex, dept]) |&gt;
  mutate(
    sex = factor(levels(df$sex)[sex], levels = levels(df$sex)),
    dept = factor(levels(df$dept)[dept], levels = levels(df$dept))
  ) |&gt;
  left_join(cells, by = c("sex", "dept")) |&gt;
  mutate(predicted = rbinom(n(), applicants, p) / applicants) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = dept, y = predicted, color = sex)) +
  stat_pointinterval(
    position = position_dodge(width = 0.5),
    .width = c(0.5, 0.9)
  ) +
  geom_point(
    data = cells,
    aes(y = observed),
    shape = 4,
    size = 3,
    stroke = 1.2,
    position = position_dodge(width = 0.5)
  ) +
  labs(
    x = "Department",
    y = "Admission rate",
    title = "Posterior predictive check",
    subtitle = "Intervals: model predictions. ×: observed rates."
  )









For most departments, the observed rates (×) fall inside the predicted intervals. Department A is the exception. Women there are admitted 82% of the time, well above anything the model predicts. The model can’t capture this because in Step 4 we assumed the sex effect is the same in every department, and department A breaks that pattern.
This is the posterior predictive check doing its job. It tells us exactly where the model is too simple. In a full analysis, we’d go back to Step 4 and let the sex effect vary by department (an interaction). For this demonstration we’ll keep the simpler model, but we’ll keep the misfit in mind: our estimate is an average over departments that don’t all behave alike.



Step 8: Compute the estimand and test its robustness
Now we can answer the question from Step 1. We compute the direct effect for every posterior draw:


Show code
de &lt;- direct_effect(fit)

de |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = direct_effect)) +
  stat_halfeye(fill = "#1b91ff") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed") +
  labs(
    x = "Direct effect (female − male)",
    y = NULL,
    title = "Posterior for the estimand"
  )









Show code
de |&gt;
  summarise(
    median = median(direct_effect),
    lower_90 = quantile(direct_effect, 0.05),
    upper_90 = quantile(direct_effect, 0.95),
    prob_women_disadvantaged = mean(direct_effect &lt; 0)
  )







Among applicants to the same department, there’s no sign that women are admitted less often than men. The best estimate is that they’re admitted about 2 percentage points more often. The 90% interval runs from about −1 to +4 points, so the data are consistent with a small advantage for women or no difference at all. The large overall gap comes from where women apply, not from how departments treat them. That’s the pattern our Step 3 simulation produced in a world with no discrimination.
A result is only as good as the assumptions behind it, so we test how much it depends on them.

Sensitivity to priors
We refit with a much tighter and a much looser prior on the sex effect. Each variant is the same model with one line changed: the scale in the prior on beta.


Show code
# a copy of the model with the prior on beta set to Normal(0, beta_sd)
with_beta_prior &lt;- function(beta_sd) {
  code &lt;- readLines("models/berkeley.stan")
  line &lt;- grep("beta ~ normal(0, 1 *", code, fixed = TRUE)
  stopifnot(length(line) == 1)
  code[line] &lt;- sub(
    "normal(0, 1 *",
    paste0("normal(0, ", beta_sd, " *"),
    code[line],
    fixed = TRUE
  )
  cmdstan_model(write_stan_file(code, dir = ".stancache"))
}

prior_scenarios &lt;- list(
  "Tight (beta ~ Normal(0, 0.1))" = with_beta_prior(0.1),
  "Default (beta ~ Normal(0, 1))" = berkeley,
  "Loose (beta ~ Normal(0, 2.5))" = with_beta_prior(2.5)
)

prior_sensitivity &lt;- prior_scenarios |&gt;
  imap(\(model, name) {
    direct_effect(fit_model(df, model = model)) |&gt; mutate(scenario = name)
  }) |&gt;
  list_rbind()




Show code
prior_sensitivity |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = direct_effect, y = scenario)) +
  stat_halfeye(fill = "#1b91ff") +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed") +
  labs(
    x = "Direct effect (female − male)",
    y = NULL,
    title = "Sensitivity to priors"
  )









The three posteriors are almost identical. Even a prior that strongly insists sex doesn’t matter barely moves the estimate. This confirms what we expected in Step 6: with thousands of applicants, the data outweigh the overly wide priors. With a small sample, this check would matter much more.


Sensitivity to the DAG: an unobserved confounder
The strongest assumption we made is that the factors left out of the DAG aren’t related to sex: men and women who apply are equally qualified. Suppose that’s wrong. Suppose women face barriers before they apply, so only the most qualified women apply at all. Then, within any department, the women are more qualified than the men. Comparing within departments would make women look favored even if departments discriminate against them.
We can see how much this matters by going back to the Step 3 simulation. We build in real discrimination (−0.5 log-odds), make qualifications matter for admission, and vary how much more qualified the female applicants are:


Show code
confounding &lt;- tibble(female_selection = c(0, 0.5, 1)) |&gt;
  mutate(
    sim = map(female_selection, \(k) {
      simulate_admissions(
        discrimination = -0.5,
        u_on_admit = 1,
        female_selection = k
      )
    }),
    truth = map_dbl(sim, \(s) {
      true_direct_effect(s, discrimination = -0.5, u_on_admit = 1)
    }),
    estimate = map(sim, \(s) direct_effect(fit_model(s), data = s))
  )




Show code
confounding |&gt;
  select(female_selection, truth, estimate) |&gt;
  unnest(estimate) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = direct_effect, y = factor(female_selection))) +
  stat_halfeye(fill = "#1b91ff") +
  geom_point(
    data = distinct(confounding, female_selection, truth),
    aes(x = truth),
    color = "red",
    shape = 4,
    size = 4,
    stroke = 1.5
  ) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed") +
  geom_vline(
    xintercept = median(de$direct_effect),
    color = "#1b91ff",
    linetype = "dotted"
  ) +
  labs(
    x = "Estimated direct effect (female − male)",
    y = "How much more qualified female applicants are (SD)",
    title = "Real discrimination, hidden by an unobserved confounder",
    subtitle = "Red ×: true effect. Dotted line: our estimate from the real data."
  )









In every one of these simulations, departments discriminate against women (red ×). When applicants are equally qualified, the model finds the discrimination. If women applicants are half a standard deviation more qualified, the estimate shrinks to less than half the true effect. At a full standard deviation, women look clearly favored, by even more than our real-data estimate (dotted line).
So our real-data result, “no direct disadvantage for women”, is credible only to the extent that we believe male and female applicants are similarly qualified. The model can’t settle that question. It’s an argument about the world, and settling it would take more data, such as measures of applicant qualifications.


The result
Within departments, we find no evidence that women are disadvantaged. If anything, they’re admitted about 2 percentage points more often. The conclusion holds up across a wide range of priors. It comes with two caveats. First, the model averages over departments, and department A doesn’t fit that average well. Second, it rests on an assumption we can’t test with this data: that male and female applicants are similarly qualified. The overall gap is real. It runs through department choice, so the place to look is why women apply to more selective departments.

